---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="What Not to Share With AI - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="what-not-to-share">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				When using most AI tools, it is important to remember that every prompt you send leaves your machine
				and travels to a server outside your direct control. Unless you are running a local model or using a
				strictly sandboxed internal company tool, you lose control over who can access that data, how securely
				it is stored, and whether it might be used to train future models. This page is about the specific things
				that should never go into an AI prompt, and why.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Rule of Thumb">
				<p>
					In general, if you are using a public or unapproved AI tool, and you would redact the info before
					posting in a public Slack message, do not put it in an AI prompt. That one test covers most of the cases below.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>API Keys and Secrets</h3>

			<p>
				Never paste API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, or connection strings into an AI
				chat. This applies to <strong>all</strong> AI tools: Claude, Gemini, ChatGPT, Copilot, Cursor, and
				even officially approved, internal enterprise tools.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">What counts as a secret</div>
					<ul class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
						<li>API keys (AWS, Stripe, Google Cloud, any third-party service)</li>
						<li>Database connection strings with credentials</li>
						<li>JWT secrets and session signing keys</li>
						<li>Private SSH or TLS keys</li>
						<li>OAuth client secrets</li>
						<li>Passwords of any kind</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				If you need the AI to help you write code that uses a secret, use a placeholder. Write{" "}
				<code>YOUR_API_KEY_HERE</code> or reference an environment variable name like{" "}
				<code>process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code>. The AI does not need the actual value to help
				you with the code.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Instead of this
				</div>
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					Here's my Stripe key: sk_live_4xKf9mN2... can you help me set up the payment flow?
				</p>
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 mt-4">
					Do this
				</div>
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					I'm using Stripe. The key will come from process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY. Help me set up
					the payment flow.
				</p>
			</div>

			<h3>Personal and Client Data</h3>

			<p>
			    Personally identifiable information (PII) about real people should <strong>never</strong>{" "}
				go into AI prompts. This includes data about your clients, your users, and your colleagues.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Examples of PII to avoid</div>
					<ul class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
						<li>Real names combined with email addresses, phone numbers, or addresses</li>
						<li>Health information, financial records, or government ID numbers</li>
						<li>Client lists with contact details</li>
						<li>User data exported from production databases</li>
						<li>HR records or performance reviews</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				If you need the AI to help with a data format, schema, or analysis task, use anonymized
				sample data. Replace real names with "John Doe," real emails with{" "}
				<code>user@example.com</code>, and real IDs with sequential numbers. The AI can help you
				with the structure without seeing real records.
			</p>

			<h3>Confidential Business Information</h3>

			<p>
				Strategy documents, unreleased product plans, M&amp;A discussions, pricing decisions, and
				competitive intelligence are all highly sensitive. In general, this information should not
				be put into AI prompts. Acceptable AI usage can vary from project to project.
				Consult with your project lead if you have specific questions.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Examples of confidential business information
					</div>
					<ul class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
						<li>Unreleased product roadmaps or feature plans</li>
						<li>Financial projections or internal cost data</li>
						<li>Acquisition targets or partnership discussions</li>
						<li>Competitive strategy documents</li>
						<li>Internal pricing models or margin data</li>
						<li>Legal matters or pending litigation details</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				For writing tasks involving confidential content outside of approved enterprise tools, consider whether you can remove the
				sensitive details, get the structure right with the AI, and fill in the real content yourself.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="Konrad AI Use and Governance Policy">
				<p>
				    Information on acceptable AI use at Konrad can also be found in the Handbook{" "}
					<a href="https://handbook.kgportal.com/232/AI_Use_and_Governance_Policy" target="_blank">here</a>.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Where Your Data Goes</h3>

			<p>
				The data handling rules differ between consumer and enterprise accounts:
			</p>

			<p>
				<strong>Consumer and free-tier accounts</strong> (personal Gmail accounts on Gemini, free ChatGPT, free
				Claude.ai) may have your conversations used to improve the model. The terms vary by
				provider and change over time, but the default on free accounts is often more permissive
				than most people realize.
			</p>

			<p>
				<strong>Enterprise and API accounts</strong> operate under different terms. Providers like Anthropic,
				Google, and OpenAI all offer enterprise tiers where customer data is explicitly not used
				for model training.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Even enterprise accounts store data">
				<p>
					Even on accounts where your data is not used for training, your prompts are transmitted
					to and processed on third-party servers. They are stored temporarily and are subject to
					the provider's security practices, as well as internal IT auditing.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>A Practical Checklist</h3>

			<div class="space-y-3 my-8">
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Know the difference between "Approved" and "Unapproved" tools.</strong>{" "}
                        Approved tools such as Konrad's pro Gemini subscription should be used when using AI at work.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Replace secrets with placeholders</strong> before pasting
						any code or config that might contain credentials. This applies to <em>all</em> AI tools, even approved ones.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Anonymize sample data</strong> when asking for help with
						data processing, schemas, or exports on unapproved platforms.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Apply the public Slack test.</strong> When using public/consumer AI,
                        if you would redact it before sharing it publicly inside the company, do not put it in a prompt.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Check the{" "}
						    <a href="https://handbook.kgportal.com/232/AI_Use_and_Governance_Policy" target="_blank">
								Konrad AI Use and Governance Policy
							</a>
						</strong>{" "}
						if you are unsure. It exists for exactly this situation. If you are still unsure, ask your project lead.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
